            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#*In basic terms, [[Feel Safe Around Electricity|electricity]] naturally tries to flow to the earth or ground through any and all materials that will conduct an electrical current.<ref>https://electronics.stackexchange.com/questions/152291/how-a-person-standing-on-a-floor-tile-and-holding-a-live-equipment-can-complete</ref>",
                    "#*Certain compounds, like wood and glass, are poor conductors of electricity. Other materials, like seawater and many metals, conduct very well. The human body is able to conduct a current largely due to the amount of sodium and water in the body, and an electric shock happens when electricity flows through parts of the body.<ref>https://electronics.stackexchange.com/questions/152291/how-a-person-standing-on-a-floor-tile-and-holding-a-live-equipment-can-complete</ref>"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#*In basic terms, [[Feel Safe Around Electricity|electricity]] naturally tries to flow to the earth or ground through any and all materials that will conduct an electrical current.<ref>https://electronics.stackexchange.com/questions/152291/how-grounding-works-to-prevent-electrical-shock</ref>",
                    "#*Certain compounds, like wood and glass, are poor conductors of electricity. Other materials, like seawater and many metals, conduct very well. The human body is able to conduct a current largely due to the amount of sodium and water in the body, and an electric shock happens when electricity flows through parts of the body.<ref>https://electronics.stackexchange.com/questions/152291/how-grounding-works-to-prevent-electrical-shock</ref>"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "32427225",
            "user": "AppTech",
            "timestamp": "17:22, 10 April 2024",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(18,956 bytes)",
            "change": 1,
            "summary": "(Fixing misspellings via the Spellchecker tool)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "cleanup",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#*Ground your appliances. The ground prong provides a quick electrical return in the event that electricity flows where it is not supposed to. This will often trigger overcurrent protection and de-energize the source before a fire can start. The ground wire should be connected to any external casing and to the ground on the outlet. Inside, ground should have almost no electrical potential to neutral and should be connected to metal pipes, metal beams, to a grounding rod outside, etc. to redirect current away from people in the event of a fault.<ref>https://www.thespruce.com/what-is-grounding-1152859</ref> Most safety equipment such as overcurrent breakers and GFCI breakers do not function correctly if there is no ground connection."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#*Ground your appliances. The ground prong provides a quick electrical return in the event that electricity flows where it is not supposed to. This will often trigger overcurrent protection and deenergize the source before a fire can start. The ground wire should be connected to any external casing and to the ground on the outlet. Inside, ground should have almost no electrical potential to neutral and should be connected to metal pipes, metal beams, to a grounding rod outside, etc. to redirect current away from people in the event of a fault.<ref>https://www.thespruce.com/what-is-grounding-1152859</ref> Most safety equipment such as overcurrent breakers and GFCI breakers do not function correctly if there is no ground connection."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "32425181",
            "user": "Awesome Aasim",
            "timestamp": "19:49, 9 April 2024",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(18,955 bytes)",
            "change": 851,
            "summary": "(rephrase info about ground + rmv misguided advice about GFCI - GFCI functions internally when there is a difference in current going out to current going in. They do not function as overcurrent devices except in the case of RCBO.)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#* A GFCI breaker provides both overcurrent and ground fault combination; these are often referred to as \"residual current circuit breaker with overcurrent protection\" or RCBO outside of North America.",
                    "#* Test GFCI outlets regularly. When you press the \"TEST\" button the breaker should trip. Confirm that the circuit is indeed dead by plugging a tester in. After resetting, current should return.",
                    "#*Ground your appliances. The ground prong provides a quick electrical return in the event that electricity flows where it is not supposed to. This will often trigger overcurrent protection and deenergize the source before a fire can start. The ground wire should be connected to any external casing and to the ground on the outlet. Inside, ground should have almost no electrical potential to neutral and should be connected to metal pipes, metal beams, to a grounding rod outside, etc. to redirect current away from people in the event of a fault.<ref>https://www.thespruce.com/what-is-grounding-1152859</ref> Most safety equipment such as overcurrent breakers and GFCI breakers do not function correctly if there is no ground connection."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#*Do not use GFCI for inductive or capacitive loads such as heavy-duty electrical motors or space heaters. These will trigger the GFCI and disconnect the load.",
                    "#*Utilize three-prong plugs whenever possible. The third prong, which grounds the electrical current, should never be removed."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "32406638",
            "user": "WikiHow Projects",
            "timestamp": "05:16, 2 April 2024",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(18,104 bytes)",
            "change": -505,
            "summary": "(removing broken link)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Turn the electricity off. The first step that you need to take before attempting to fix any electrical problems yourself is to turn the electricity off in your home. This helps to ensure that, even if you make a mistake, you won't be electrocuted.[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 4 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* There will be a main electrical panel somewhere in your home (usually in a basement or garage). This panel has a simple on/off switch that allows you to cut the flow of electricity to your entire house. Make sure the switch on this panel is flipped to “off” before attempting any repairs.",
                    "#Turn the power back on. Once you have made the necessary repairs and are ready to test the appliance or outlet you have fixed, turn the power switch on your main panel back to the “on” position. [[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 10 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* You may need to reset your circuit breakers as well. To do this, flip the switch on each individual breaker to “off”, then flip it back to “on”.",
                    "#Turn the power source off. Whenever a project involves exposure to electrical equipment or electricity, check and double-check that the power is off before beginning your work.[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 13 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Again, there should be a main electric panel for the entire facility. Find this panel and flip the switch to off."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Turn the electricity off. The first step that you need to take before attempting to fix any electrical problems yourself is to turn the electricity off in your home. This helps to ensure that, even if you make a mistake, you won't be electrocuted.<ref>https://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 4 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* There will be a main electrical panel somewhere in your home (usually in a basement or garage). This panel has a simple on/off switch that allows you to cut the flow of electricity to your entire house. Make sure the switch on this panel is flipped to “off” before attempting any repairs.<ref>https://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page</ref>",
                    "#Turn the power back on. Once you have made the necessary repairs and are ready to test the appliance or outlet you have fixed, turn the power switch on your main panel back to the “on” position. <ref>https://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page?</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 10 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* You may need to reset your circuit breakers as well. To do this, flip the switch on each individual breaker to “off”, then flip it back to “on”.<ref> https://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page?</ref>",
                    "#Turn the power source off. Whenever a project involves exposure to electrical equipment or electricity, check and double-check that the power is off before beginning your work.<ref> https://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page?</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 13 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Again, there should be a main electric panel for the entire facility. Find this panel and flip the switch to off.<ref>https://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page?</ref>"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "32406636",
