            "contribution_type": "deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Double up. It is always wise to have a second person around to assist you when working with electricity. This second person can double check to ensure you have followed all necessary precautions. Also, if something does go wrong and you do get shocked, this second person can immediately get you the help you need.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/etools/electric-power</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 16 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Make sure you communicate well with this other person. Many electrical accidents occur because of miscommunication. You need to be able to trust that when this person says the power is off, it is actually off.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/etools/electric-power</ref>",
                    "#* Even if you trust this other person with your life, it is probably a good idea to double check and make sure the power is off for yourself. Never assume anything when dealing with electricity.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/etools/electric-power</ref>",
                    "#Prepare for the worst. No matter how many precautions are in place, [[Treat a Victim of Electrical Shock|electric shock]] is always a risk when electricity is in use. If shock occurs, it is important to be prepared in order to handle the situation safely.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/etools/electric-power</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 23 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Call for help. In the case of an electric emergency, always  [[Call Emergency Services|call the Emergency Services]]. It is unwise to try to treat the victim yourself when an electrical shock has occurred.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/etools/electric-power</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 24 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Check for vitals. Once you are certain the victim is no longer conducting electricity, check to see if the person is breathing. If the victim is not breathing, begin [[Do CPR on an Adult|CPR]] immediately while someone else alerts emergency medical services.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/etools/electric-power</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 27.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* OSHA's safety rules for working around electricity state that you have about 4 minutes to get help for an electric shock victim, so move quickly.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/etools/electric-power</ref>"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Double up. It is always wise to have a second person around to assist you when working with electricity. This second person can double check to ensure you have followed all necessary precautions. Also, if something does go wrong and you do get shocked, this second person can immediately get you the help you need.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/electric_power/medical_first_aid.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 16 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Make sure you communicate well with this other person. Many electrical accidents occur because of miscommunication. You need to be able to trust that when this person says the power is off, it is actually off.<ref> https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/electric_power/medical_first_aid.html</ref>",
                    "#* Even if you trust this other person with your life, it is probably a good idea to double check and make sure the power is off for yourself. Never assume anything when dealing with electricity.<ref> https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/electric_power/medical_first_aid.html</ref>",
                    "#Prepare for the worst. No matter how many precautions are in place, [[Treat a Victim of Electrical Shock|electric shock]] is always a risk when electricity is in use. If shock occurs, it is important to be prepared in order to handle the situation safely.<ref> https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/electric_power/medical_first_aid.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 23 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Call for help. In the case of an electric emergency, always  [[Call Emergency Services|call the Emergency Services]]. It is unwise to try to treat the victim yourself when an electrical shock has occurred.<ref> https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/electric_power/medical_first_aid.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 24 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Check for vitals. Once you are certain the victim is no longer conducting electricity, check to see if the person is breathing. If the victim is not breathing, begin [[Do CPR on an Adult|CPR]] immediately while someone else alerts emergency medical services.<ref> https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/electric_power/medical_first_aid.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 27.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* OSHA's safety rules for working around electricity state that you have about 4 minutes to get help for an electric shock victim, so move quickly.<ref> https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/electric_power/medical_first_aid.html</ref>"
                ]
            }
        },
