            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Know your limits. There are some simple electrical problems around the house that you can handle yourself. However, any time you are having serious or major electrical issues, you should hire a professional electrician. It may be expensive, but it is cheaper than a stint in the hospital.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/electrical/21015548/bringing-in-an-electrician</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Know your limits. There are some simple electrical problems around the house that you can handle yourself. However, any time you are having serious or major electrical issues, you should hire a professional electrician. It may be expensive, but it is cheaper than a stint in the hospital.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/toh/article/0,,213817,00.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "32128060",
            "user": "WikiHow Horizon 3",
            "timestamp": "20:59, 10 December 2023",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(18,816 bytes)",
            "change": 21,
            "summary": "(http ► https)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#*In basic terms, [[Feel Safe Around Electricity|electricity]] naturally tries to flow to the earth or ground through any and all materials that will conduct an electrical current.<ref>https://electronics.stackexchange.com/questions/152291/how-grounding-works-to-prevent-electrical-shock</ref>",
                    "#*Certain compounds, like wood and glass, are poor conductors of electricity. Other materials, like seawater and many metals, conduct very well. The human body is able to conduct a current largely due to the amount of sodium and water in the body, and an electric shock happens when electricity flows through parts of the body.<ref>https://electronics.stackexchange.com/questions/152291/how-grounding-works-to-prevent-electrical-shock</ref>",
                    "#Know your limits. There are some simple electrical problems around the house that you can handle yourself. However, any time you are having serious or major electrical issues, you should hire a professional electrician. It may be expensive, but it is cheaper than a stint in the hospital.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/toh/article/0,,213817,00.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn the electricity off. The first step that you need to take before attempting to fix any electrical problems yourself is to turn the electricity off in your home. This helps to ensure that, even if you make a mistake, you won't be electrocuted.<ref>https://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 4 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* There will be a main electrical panel somewhere in your home (usually in a basement or garage). This panel has a simple on/off switch that allows you to cut the flow of electricity to your entire house. Make sure the switch on this panel is flipped to “off” before attempting any repairs.<ref>https://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page</ref>",
                    "#Cover sockets and outlets. Covering outlets with wall panels is crucial for preventing accidental contact with wires, and is required by code. If you live with small children, it is also wise to use socket safety plugs to keep curious fingers safe from injury.<ref>https://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 5 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Install GFCI breakers and outlets. GFCI, or Ground Fault Circuit Interrupter, devices are able to detect imbalances in the amount of electricity flowing through a circuit and will cut off power at the GFCI device. GFCI receptacles are required in most new construction homes in places where electric shock is most likely – near sinks, in the bathroom, over kitchen counters, in the garage, and outdoors – and can typically be installed in older homes for a low cost.<ref> https://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 6 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Avoid overloading power strips and other receptacles with multiple plugs. Using only two plugs per outlet lessens the risk of shock and fire.<ref> https://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>",
                    "#*Never use an electrical appliance while in the bath or shower.<ref> https://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>",
                    "#*If your toaster or another electric appliance is near your kitchen sink, never use the running water and the appliance at the same time. Keep it unplugged when not in use.<ref> https://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>",
                    "#* Store outdoor electrical equipment in a place where it will be kept dry, like a garage shelf.<ref> https://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>",
                    "#*If a plugged-in appliance falls into water, do not attempt to retrieve it until you turn off power to the corresponding circuit. Once the power is off, you may retrieve the device. Once it is dry, it can be evaluated by an electrician to see if it is suitable for future use.<ref> https://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>",
                    "#Replace worn or damaged equipment. Pay attention to the condition of your electric devices, and maintain them regularly. Some signs that indicate a need for repair are:<ref> https://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 9 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn the power back on. Once you have made the necessary repairs and are ready to test the appliance or outlet you have fixed, turn the power switch on your main panel back to the “on” position. <ref>https://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page?</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 10 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* You may need to reset your circuit breakers as well. To do this, flip the switch on each individual breaker to “off”, then flip it back to “on”.<ref> https://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page?</ref>",
                    "#Turn the power source off. Whenever a project involves exposure to electrical equipment or electricity, check and double-check that the power is off before beginning your work.<ref> https://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page?</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 13 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Again, there should be a main electric panel for the entire facility. Find this panel and flip the switch to off.<ref>https://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page?</ref>",
                    "#Do not touch a victim of shock with bare hands. Shock victims usually don't hold the electricity in their bodies for very long. However, you must always use caution, as the victim could potentially still be conducting electricity. Use a non-conductive barrier, like rubber gloves, if at all possible, to touch or move the victim.<ref>https://www.mayoclinic.org/first-aid/first-aid-electrical-shock/basics/art-20056695</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 25 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn off the power source, if possible. If you can do so without being shocked yourself, turn off the power. If this is not possible, move the victim away from the source with a non-conductive material, like a piece of wood.<ref> https://www.mayoclinic.org/first-aid/first-aid-electrical-shock/basics/art-20056695</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 26 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* You should only try to move an electrical shock victim if the person is in immediate danger.<ref>https://www.mayoclinic.org/first-aid/first-aid-electrical-shock/basics/art-20056695</ref>",
                    "#Wait for medical help to arrive. Stay calm and keep the victim lying horizontally, with his legs slightly elevated until medical help can arrive. Once help arrives, stay out of the paramedics' way. If the paramedics ask for any assistance, follow their instructions.<ref> https://www.mayoclinic.org/first-aid/first-aid-electrical-shock/basics/art-20056695</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 28.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#*In basic terms, [[Feel Safe Around Electricity|electricity]] naturally tries to flow to the earth or ground through any and all materials that will conduct an electrical current.<ref>http://electronics.stackexchange.com/questions/152291/how-grounding-works-to-prevent-electrical-shock</ref>",
                    "#*Certain compounds, like wood and glass, are poor conductors of electricity. Other materials, like seawater and many metals, conduct very well. The human body is able to conduct a current largely due to the amount of sodium and water in the body, and an electric shock happens when electricity flows through parts of the body.<ref>http://electronics.stackexchange.com/questions/152291/how-grounding-works-to-prevent-electrical-shock</ref>",
                    "#Know your limits. There are some simple electrical problems around the house that you can handle yourself. However, any time you are having serious or major electrical issues, you should hire a professional electrician. It may be expensive, but it is cheaper than a stint in the hospital.<ref>http://www.thisoldhouse.com/toh/article/0,,213817,00.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn the electricity off. The first step that you need to take before attempting to fix any electrical problems yourself is to turn the electricity off in your home. This helps to ensure that, even if you make a mistake, you won't be electrocuted.<ref>http://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 4 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* There will be a main electrical panel somewhere in your home (usually in a basement or garage). This panel has a simple on/off switch that allows you to cut the flow of electricity to your entire house. Make sure the switch on this panel is flipped to “off” before attempting any repairs.<ref>http://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page</ref>",
                    "#Cover sockets and outlets. Covering outlets with wall panels is crucial for preventing accidental contact with wires, and is required by code. If you live with small children, it is also wise to use socket safety plugs to keep curious fingers safe from injury.<ref>http://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 5 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Install GFCI breakers and outlets. GFCI, or Ground Fault Circuit Interrupter, devices are able to detect imbalances in the amount of electricity flowing through a circuit and will cut off power at the GFCI device. GFCI receptacles are required in most new construction homes in places where electric shock is most likely – near sinks, in the bathroom, over kitchen counters, in the garage, and outdoors – and can typically be installed in older homes for a low cost.<ref> http://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 6 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Avoid overloading power strips and other receptacles with multiple plugs. Using only two plugs per outlet lessens the risk of shock and fire.<ref> http://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>",
                    "#*Never use an electrical appliance while in the bath or shower.<ref> http://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>",
                    "#*If your toaster or another electric appliance is near your kitchen sink, never use the running water and the appliance at the same time. Keep it unplugged when not in use.<ref> http://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>",
                    "#* Store outdoor electrical equipment in a place where it will be kept dry, like a garage shelf.<ref> http://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>",
                    "#*If a plugged-in appliance falls into water, do not attempt to retrieve it until you turn off power to the corresponding circuit. Once the power is off, you may retrieve the device. Once it is dry, it can be evaluated by an electrician to see if it is suitable for future use.<ref> http://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>",
                    "#Replace worn or damaged equipment. Pay attention to the condition of your electric devices, and maintain them regularly. Some signs that indicate a need for repair are:<ref> http://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 9 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn the power back on. Once you have made the necessary repairs and are ready to test the appliance or outlet you have fixed, turn the power switch on your main panel back to the “on” position. <ref>http://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page?</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 10 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* You may need to reset your circuit breakers as well. To do this, flip the switch on each individual breaker to “off”, then flip it back to “on”.<ref> http://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page?</ref>",
                    "#Turn the power source off. Whenever a project involves exposure to electrical equipment or electricity, check and double-check that the power is off before beginning your work.<ref> http://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page?</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 13 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Again, there should be a main electric panel for the entire facility. Find this panel and flip the switch to off.<ref>http://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page?</ref>",
                    "#Do not touch a victim of shock with bare hands. Shock victims usually don't hold the electricity in their bodies for very long. However, you must always use caution, as the victim could potentially still be conducting electricity. Use a non-conductive barrier, like rubber gloves, if at all possible, to touch or move the victim.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/first-aid/first-aid-electrical-shock/basics/art-20056695</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 25 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn off the power source, if possible. If you can do so without being shocked yourself, turn off the power. If this is not possible, move the victim away from the source with a non-conductive material, like a piece of wood.<ref> http://www.mayoclinic.org/first-aid/first-aid-electrical-shock/basics/art-20056695</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 26 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* You should only try to move an electrical shock victim if the person is in immediate danger.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/first-aid/first-aid-electrical-shock/basics/art-20056695</ref>",
                    "#Wait for medical help to arrive. Stay calm and keep the victim lying horizontally, with his legs slightly elevated until medical help can arrive. Once help arrives, stay out of the paramedics' way. If the paramedics ask for any assistance, follow their instructions.<ref> http://www.mayoclinic.org/first-aid/first-aid-electrical-shock/basics/art-20056695</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 28.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
