            "contribution_type": "deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Learn how electricity works. The first step in preventing a dangerous situation is to understand the cause of electric shock. Read books, articles, websites and blogs about electricity and safety measures to take when working with electricity.[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 1 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*This happens most often when a direct source of electricity is exposed to human contact. It may also flow into a person through another conductor, like a pool of water or a metal pole.",
                    "#Call a professional for big jobs. Working with electricity is inherently dangerous and complicated. If you are not completely confident in your skills, bring in a trusted electrician to complete the job.[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 17 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Keep a [[Use a Fire Extinguisher|fire extinguisher]] handy. Keep a fire extinguisher ready to go in areas where you work with electrical equipment. Fire extinguishers for use on electrical fires will have a \"C,\" \"BC,\" or \"ABC\" on the label.[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 22 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Learn how electricity works. The first step in preventing a dangerous situation is to understand the cause of electric shock. Read books, articles, websites and blogs about electricity and safety measures to take when working with electricity.<ref> http://www.safetyed.org/howtopreventelecrticalshock.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 1 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*This happens most often when a direct source of electricity is exposed to human contact. It may also flow into a person through another conductor, like a pool of water or a metal pole.<ref> http://www.safetyed.org/howtopreventelecrticalshock.html</ref>",
                    "#Call a professional for big jobs. Working with electricity is inherently dangerous and complicated. If you are not completely confident in your skills, bring in a trusted electrician to complete the job.<ref>http://www.safetyed.org/howtopreventelecrticalshock.html</ref> [[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 17 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Keep a [[Use a Fire Extinguisher|fire extinguisher]] handy. Keep a fire extinguisher ready to go in areas where you work with electrical equipment. Fire extinguishers for use on electrical fires will have a \"C,\" \"BC,\" or \"ABC\" on the label.<ref>http://www.safetyed.org/howtopreventelecrticalshock.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 22 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
