            "id": "26145920",
            "user": "WikiHow Projects",
            "timestamp": "09:59, 9 March 2019",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(17,981 bytes)",
            "change": 120,
            "summary": "(→‎Steps:  added 1.11, 1.12)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Consider installing safety outlets and outlet covers.  Safety outlets require some force to expose the female connector; outlet covers plug into the female connector of the outlet, do not conduct electricity, and are hard to remove for most young children.[[Image:Child Proof a Living Room Step 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Be sure that the appliance is set at the appropriate settings for the outlet you are plugging in.  In a country that uses a higher voltage, there is a higher wattage, which can damage your electronics and risk electrical shock.  Make sure that the appliance is set to the appropriate voltage input, and use a 100-240V to 110V or 220V transformer to avoid mistakes caused by accidentally plugging in appliances into the wrong voltage.[[Image:Maintain-Appliances-to-Conserve-Energy-Step-20.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Consider installing safety outlets and outlet covers.  Safety outlets require some force to expose the female connector; outlet covers plug into the female connector of the outlet, do not conduct electricity, and are hard to remove for most young children.",
                    "#Be sure that the appliance is set at the appropriate settings for the outlet you are plugging in.  In a country that uses a higher voltage, there is a higher wattage, which can damage your electronics and risk electrical shock.  Make sure that the appliance is set to the appropriate voltage input, and use a 100-240V to 110V or 220V transformer to avoid mistakes caused by accidentally plugging in appliances into the wrong voltage."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "25941925",
            "user": "MiscBot",
            "timestamp": "12:04, 28 January 2019",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(17,861 bytes)",
            "change": -525,
            "summary": "(Automatic link maintenance: cleaning up links to references that are broken)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "sourcing",
            "reverted_by": "Agusbou2015",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Check the weather report. It may sound obvious, but making sure you have a clear forecast for your outdoor adventure is crucial to avoid getting caught in an electrical storm. Even if you're just gone for an afternoon, weather can change quickly and the best prevention is preparedness. Know the chance of a thunderstorm in the outdoor area you plan to visit, and plan to head in long before the [[Avoid Being Hit by Lightning|lightning]] is due to start.[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 16.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Watch for signs of a coming storm. Pay attention to changes in temperature, increases in wind, or darkening of the sky. Listen for thunder. If it looks like a storm is rolling in, stop what you are doing and take shelter immediately. [[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 17.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find shelter. If you are outside and a storm approaches, quickly heading indoors is the only real way to be protected from lightning. Look for a completely enclosed shelter with its own electricity and plumbing, like a house or business. If that option is not available, hiding out in a car with the doors closed and windows up is also a safe bet. Covered picnic areas, stand-alone restroom facilities, tents, and other small structures will not keep you safe. No reliable shelter in sight? Reduce your risk with these preventive guidelines:[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 18.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Stay low",
                    "#*Avoid open areas",
                    "#*Avoid metal and water",
                    "#Wait it out. Whether indoors or out, do not leave your chosen safety area for at least half an hour after the last clap of thunder is heard. If you have any doubts about whether or not the storm has passed, stay inside.  [[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 19.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Check the weather report. It may sound obvious, but making sure you have a clear forecast for your outdoor adventure is crucial to avoid getting caught in an electrical storm. Even if you're just gone for an afternoon, weather can change quickly and the best prevention is preparedness. Know the chance of a thunderstorm in the outdoor area you plan to visit, and plan to head in long before the [[Avoid Being Hit by Lightning|lightning]] is due to start.<ref>http://www.publicaffairs.noaa.gov/grounders/lightningsafety.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 16.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Watch for signs of a coming storm. Pay attention to changes in temperature, increases in wind, or darkening of the sky. Listen for thunder. If it looks like a storm is rolling in, stop what you are doing and take shelter immediately. <ref>http://www.publicaffairs.noaa.gov/grounders/lightningsafety.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 17.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find shelter. If you are outside and a storm approaches, quickly heading indoors is the only real way to be protected from lightning. Look for a completely enclosed shelter with its own electricity and plumbing, like a house or business. If that option is not available, hiding out in a car with the doors closed and windows up is also a safe bet. Covered picnic areas, stand-alone restroom facilities, tents, and other small structures will not keep you safe. No reliable shelter in sight? Reduce your risk with these preventive guidelines:<ref>http://www.publicaffairs.noaa.gov/grounders/lightningsafety.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 18.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Stay low<ref>http://www.publicaffairs.noaa.gov/grounders/lightningsafety.html</ref>",
                    "#*Avoid open areas<ref>http://www.publicaffairs.noaa.gov/grounders/lightningsafety.html</ref>",
                    "#*Avoid metal and water<ref>http://www.publicaffairs.noaa.gov/grounders/lightningsafety.html</ref>",
                    "#Wait it out. Whether indoors or out, do not leave your chosen safety area for at least half an hour after the last clap of thunder is heard. If you have any doubts about whether or not the storm has passed, stay inside. <ref>http://www.publicaffairs.noaa.gov/grounders/lightningsafety.html</ref> [[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 19.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
