            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "This wikiHow will teach you how to prevent electrical shock [[#step_1_1|at home]], [[#step_2_1|on the job]], and [[#step_3_1|outdoors.]]",
                    "",
                    "== 10 Second Summary ==",
                    "1. Turn the electricity off.<br>",
                    "2. Cover sockets and outlets.<br>",
                    "3. Install GFCI breakers, outlets and adapters.<br>",
                    "4. Replace worn or damaged equipment.<br>",
                    "5. Turn the power back on after repairs are done."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "*'''[[#step_1_1|In the home:]]''' Turn the electricity off and cover sockets and outlets. Install GFCI breakers, outlets and adapters, and replace worn or damaged equipment. Turn the power back on after repairs are done.",
                    "*'''[[#step_2_1|On the job:]]''' Turn off power sources and wear protective gear. Use caution when working with power tools. Have an assistant and call a professional for big jobs.",
                    "*'''[[#step_3_1|Outdoors:]]''' Find shelter indoors or in a car. Avoid covered picnic areas, tents, and stand-alone restrooms. Wait it out."
                ]
            }
        },
