        {
            "id": "19844438",
            "user": "Wikivisual",
            "timestamp": "09:31, 19 March 2016",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(17,117 bytes)",
            "change": 760,
            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "reverted_by": "R2_d2000",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Learn how electricity works. Knowledge is power, and the first step in preventing a dangerous situation is to understand the cause of electric shock. Read books, articles, websites and blogs about electricity and safety measures to take when working with electricity.<ref> http://www.safetyed.org/howtopreventelecrticalshock.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Know your limits. There are some simple electrical problems around the house that you can handle yourself. However, any time you are having serious or major electrical issues, you should hire a professional electrician. It may be expensive, but it is cheaper than a stint in the hospital.<ref>http://www.thisoldhouse.com/toh/article/0,,213817,00.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find out electricity requirements. The items and appliances in your household all have their own electrical requirements. Know the specific types of circuit breakers, fuses, and even lightbulbs that are required in your home. Make sure to replace them with the right parts when needed. Using incompatible parts could cause the equipment to function improperly, create an unsafe condition that may result in fire, injury or death.<ref>http://www.caes.uga.edu/departments/bae/extension/handbook/documents/Wiring%20Requirements%20for%20Household%20Circuits.pdf</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn the electricity off. The first step that you need to take before attempting to fix any electrical problems yourself is to turn the electricity off in your home. This helps to ensure that, even if you make a mistake, you won’t be electrocuted.<ref>http://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Cover sockets and outlets. Covering outlets with wall panels is crucial for preventing accidental contact with wires, and is required by code. If you live with small children, it is also wise to use socket safety plugs to keep curious fingers safe from injury.<ref>http://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Install GFCI breakers, outlets and adapters. GFCI, or Ground Fault Circuit Interrupter, devices are able to detect imbalances in the amount of electricity flowing through an appliance and will cut off power at the GFCI device. GFCI receptacles are required in most new construction homes, and can typically be installed in older homes for a low cost.<ref> http://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Avoid common mistakes. There are a handful of common mistakes people make when attempting to make electrical fixes in their homes. You need to be aware of these mistakes and take all the necessary precautions to avoid them. Some things to avoid are:[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Avoid water. Store and use electric devices away from water. Water and electricity do not mix well and appliances should always be kept away from any moisture. This will prevent any accidental shocks from occurring.[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 8 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Replace worn or damaged equipment. Pay attention to the condition of your electric devices, and maintain them regularly. Some signs that indicate a need for repair are:<ref> http://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn the power back on. Once you have made the necessary repairs and are ready to test the appliance or outlet you have fixed, turn the power switch on your main panel back to the “on” position. <ref>http://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page?</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 10 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn the power source off. Whenever a project involves exposure to electrical equipment or electricity, check and double-check that the power is off before beginning your work.<ref> http://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page?</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 11 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Wear protective gear. Rubber soled shoes and non-conductive gloves provide a barrier. Putting a rubber mat on the floor is another effective precaution. Rubber does not conduct electricity and will help you avoid getting shocked.<ref> https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/electric_power/personal_protective_equipment.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 12 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Exercise caution when operating power tools. Make sure that all of your tools have a three-pronged plug, and inspect all equipment for signs of damage. It is also important to turn off power tools before connecting them to electricity. Always keep power tools away from water, and clear the work area of flammable gasses, vapors, and solvents when tools are in use.<ref> https://www.osha.gov/Publications/osha3080.pdf</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 13 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Double up. It is always wise to have a second person around to assist you when working with electricity. This second person can double check to ensure you have followed all necessary precautions. Also, if something does go wrong and you do get shocked, this second person can immediately get you the help you need.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/electric_power/medical_first_aid.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 14 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Call a professional for big jobs. Working with electricity is inherently dangerous and complicated. If you are not completely confident in your skills, bring in a trusted electrician to complete the job.<ref>http://www.safetyed.org/howtopreventelecrticalshock.html</ref> [[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 15.jpg|center]]",
                    "=== Preventing Electric Shock in a Lightning Storm===",
                    "#Check the weather report. It may sound obvious, but making sure you have a clear forecast for your outdoor adventure is crucial to avoid getting caught in an electrical storm. Even if you're just gone for an afternoon, weather can change quickly and the best prevention is preparedness. Know the chance of a thunderstorm in the outdoor area you plan to visit, and plan to head in long before the [[Avoid Being Hit by Lightning|lightning]] is due to start.<ref>http://www.publicaffairs.noaa.gov/grounders/lightningsafety.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 16.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Watch for signs of a coming storm. Pay attention to changes in temperature, increases in wind, or darkening of the sky. Listen for thunder. If it looks like a storm is rolling in, stop what you are doing and take shelter immediately. <ref>http://www.publicaffairs.noaa.gov/grounders/lightningsafety.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 17.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find shelter. If you are outside and a storm approaches, quickly heading indoors is the only real way to be protected from lightning. Look for a completely enclosed shelter with its own electricity and plumbing, like a house or business. If that option is not available, hiding out in a car with the doors closed and windows up is also a safe bet. Covered picnic areas, stand-alone restroom facilities, tents, and other small structures will not keep you safe. No reliable shelter in sight? Reduce your risk with these preventive guidelines:<ref>http://www.publicaffairs.noaa.gov/grounders/lightningsafety.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 18.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Wait it out. Whether indoors or out, do not leave your chosen safety area for at least half an hour after the last clap of thunder is heard. If you have any doubts about whether or not the storm has passed, stay inside. <ref>http://www.publicaffairs.noaa.gov/grounders/lightningsafety.html</ref> [[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 19.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Keep a [[Use a Fire Extinguisher|fire extinguisher]] handy. Keep a fire extinguisher ready to go in areas where you work with electrical equipment. Fire extinguishers for use on electrical fires will have a \"C,\" \"BC,\" or \"ABC\" on the label.<ref>http://www.safetyed.org/howtopreventelecrticalshock.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 20.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Prepare for the worst. No matter how many precautions are in place, [[Treat a Victim of Electrical Shock|electric shock]] is always a risk when electricity is in use. If shock occurs, it is important to be prepared in order to handle the situation safely.<ref> https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/electric_power/medical_first_aid.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 21.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Call for help. In the case of an electric emergency, always  [[Call Emergency Services|call the Emergency Services]]. It is unwise to try to treat the victim yourself when an electrical shock has occurred.<ref> https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/electric_power/medical_first_aid.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 22.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Do not touch a victim of shock with bare hands. Shock victims usually don’t hold the electricity in their bodies for very long. However, you must always use caution, as the victim could potentially still be conducting electricity. Use a non-conductive barrier, like rubber gloves, if at all possible, to touch or move the victim.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/first-aid/first-aid-electrical-shock/basics/art-20056695</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 23.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn off the power source, if possible. If you can do so without being shocked yourself, turn off the power. If this is not possible, move the victim away from the source with a non-conductive material, like a piece of wood.<ref> http://www.mayoclinic.org/first-aid/first-aid-electrical-shock/basics/art-20056695</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 24.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Check for vitals. Once you are certain the victim is no longer conducting electricity, check to see if the person is breathing. If the victim is not breathing, begin [[Do CPR on an Adult|CPR]] immediately while someone else alerts emergency medical services.<ref> https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/electric_power/medical_first_aid.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 25.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Wait for medical help to arrive. Stay calm and keep the victim lying horizontally, with his legs slightly elevated until medical help can arrive. Once help arrives, stay out of the paramedics’ way. If the paramedics ask for any assistance, follow their instructions.<ref> http://www.mayoclinic.org/first-aid/first-aid-electrical-shock/basics/art-20056695</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 26.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Learn how electricity works. Knowledge is power, and the first step in preventing a dangerous situation is to understand the cause of electric shock. Read books, articles, websites and blogs about electricity and safety measures to take when working with electricity.<ref> http://www.safetyed.org/howtopreventelecrticalshock.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 1.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Know your limits. There are some simple electrical problems around the house that you can handle yourself. However, any time you are having serious or major electrical issues, you should hire a professional electrician. It may be expensive, but it is cheaper than a stint in the hospital.<ref>http://www.thisoldhouse.com/toh/article/0,,213817,00.html</ref>",
                    "#Find out electricity requirements. The items and appliances in your household all have their own electrical requirements. Know the specific types of circuit breakers, fuses, and even lightbulbs that are required in your home. Make sure to replace them with the right parts when needed. Using incompatible parts could cause the equipment to function improperly, create an unsafe condition that may result in fire, injury or death.<ref>http://www.caes.uga.edu/departments/bae/extension/handbook/documents/Wiring%20Requirements%20for%20Household%20Circuits.pdf</ref> [[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Turn the electricity off. The first step that you need to take before attempting to fix any electrical problems yourself is to turn the electricity off in your home. This helps to ensure that, even if you make a mistake, you won’t be electrocuted.<ref>http://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page</ref>",
                    "#Cover sockets and outlets. Covering outlets with wall panels is crucial for preventing accidental contact with wires, and is required by code. If you live with small children, it is also wise to use socket safety plugs to keep curious fingers safe from injury.<ref>http://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Install GFCI breakers, outlets and adapters. GFCI, or Ground Fault Circuit Interrupter, devices are able to detect imbalances in the amount of electricity flowing through an appliance and will cut off power at the GFCI device. GFCI receptacles are required in most new construction homes, and can typically be installed in older homes for a low cost.<ref> http://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref> [[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Avoid common mistakes. There are a handful of common mistakes people make when attempting to make electrical fixes in their homes. You need to be aware of these mistakes and take all the necessary precautions to avoid them. Some things to avoid are:",
                    "# Avoid water. Store and use electric devices away from water. Water and electricity do not mix well and appliances should always be kept away from any moisture. This will prevent any accidental shocks from occurring.[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Replace worn or damaged equipment. Pay attention to the condition of your electric devices, and maintain them regularly. Some signs that indicate a need for repair are:<ref> http://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref> [[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Turn the power back on. Once you have made the necessary repairs and are ready to test the appliance or outlet you have fixed, turn the power switch on your main panel back to the “on” position. <ref>http://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page?</ref>",
                    "#Turn the power source off. Whenever a project involves exposure to electrical equipment or electricity, check and double-check that the power is off before beginning your work.<ref> http://www.pge.com/en/safety/gaselectricsafety/electriconoff/index.page?</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 7.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Wear protective gear. Rubber soled shoes and non-conductive gloves provide a barrier. Putting a rubber mat on the floor is another effective precaution. Rubber does not conduct electricity and will help you avoid getting shocked.<ref> https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/electric_power/personal_protective_equipment.html</ref> [[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Exercise caution when operating power tools. Make sure that all of your tools have a three-pronged plug, and inspect all equipment for signs of damage. It is also important to turn off power tools before connecting them to electricity. Always keep power tools away from water, and clear the work area of flammable gasses, vapors, and solvents when tools are in use.<ref> https://www.osha.gov/Publications/osha3080.pdf</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 9.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Double up. It is always wise to have a second person around to assist you when working with electricity. This second person can double check to ensure you have followed all necessary precautions. Also, if something does go wrong and you do get shocked, this second person can immediately get you the help you need.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/electric_power/medical_first_aid.html</ref>",
                    "#Call a professional for big jobs. Working with electricity is inherently dangerous and complicated. If you are not completely confident in your skills, bring in a trusted electrician to complete the job.<ref>http://www.safetyed.org/howtopreventelecrticalshock.html</ref> [[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 10.jpg|center]]",
                    "=== Preventing Electric Shock in a Lightning Storm===",
                    "#Check the weather report. It may sound obvious, but making sure you have a clear forecast for your outdoor adventure is crucial to avoid getting caught in an electrical storm. Even if you're just gone for an afternoon, weather can change quickly and the best prevention is preparedness. Know the chance of a thunderstorm in the outdoor area you plan to visit, and plan to head in long before the [[Avoid Being Hit by Lightning|lightning]] is due to start.<ref>http://www.publicaffairs.noaa.gov/grounders/lightningsafety.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 11.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Watch for signs of a coming storm. Pay attention to changes in temperature, increases in wind, or darkening of the sky. Listen for thunder. If it looks like a storm is rolling in, stop what you are doing and take shelter immediately. <ref>http://www.publicaffairs.noaa.gov/grounders/lightningsafety.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 12.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find shelter. If you are outside and a storm approaches, quickly heading indoors is the only real way to be protected from lightning. Look for a completely enclosed shelter with its own electricity and plumbing, like a house or business. If that option is not available, hiding out in a car with the doors closed and windows up is also a safe bet. Covered picnic areas, stand-alone restroom facilities, tents, and other small structures will not keep you safe. No reliable shelter in sight? Reduce your risk with these preventive guidelines:<ref>http://www.publicaffairs.noaa.gov/grounders/lightningsafety.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 13.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Wait it out. Whether indoors or out, do not leave your chosen safety area for at least half an hour after the last clap of thunder is heard. If you have any doubts about whether or not the storm has passed, stay inside. <ref>http://www.publicaffairs.noaa.gov/grounders/lightningsafety.html</ref> [[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 14.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Keep a [[Use a Fire Extinguisher|fire extinguisher]] handy. Keep a fire extinguisher ready to go in areas where you work with electrical equipment. Fire extinguishers for use on electrical fires will have a \"C,\" \"BC,\" or \"ABC\" on the label.<ref>http://www.safetyed.org/howtopreventelecrticalshock.html</ref>",
                    "# Prepare for the worst. No matter how many precautions are in place, [[Treat a Victim of Electrical Shock|electric shock]] is always a risk when electricity is in use. If shock occurs, it is important to be prepared in order to handle the situation safely.<ref> https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/electric_power/medical_first_aid.html</ref>",
                    "# Call for help. In the case of an electric emergency, always  [[Call Emergency Services|call the Emergency Services]]. It is unwise to try to treat the victim yourself when an electrical shock has occurred.<ref> https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/electric_power/medical_first_aid.html</ref>",
                    "#Do not touch a victim of shock with bare hands. Shock victims usually don’t hold the electricity in their bodies for very long. However, you must always use caution, as the victim could potentially still be conducting electricity. Use a non-conductive barrier, like rubber gloves, if at all possible, to touch or move the victim.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/first-aid/first-aid-electrical-shock/basics/art-20056695</ref>",
                    "#Turn off the power source, if possible. If you can do so without being shocked yourself, turn off the power. If this is not possible, move the victim away from the source with a non-conductive material, like a piece of wood.<ref> http://www.mayoclinic.org/first-aid/first-aid-electrical-shock/basics/art-20056695</ref>",
                    "# Check for vitals. Once you are certain the victim is no longer conducting electricity, check to see if the person is breathing. If the victim is not breathing, begin [[Do CPR on an Adult|CPR]] immediately while someone else alerts emergency medical services.<ref> https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/electric_power/medical_first_aid.html</ref>",
                    "# Wait for medical help to arrive. Stay calm and keep the victim lying horizontally, with his legs slightly elevated until medical help can arrive. Once help arrives, stay out of the paramedics’ way. If the paramedics ask for any assistance, follow their instructions.<ref> http://www.mayoclinic.org/first-aid/first-aid-electrical-shock/basics/art-20056695</ref>"
                ]
            }
        },
