        {
            "id": "18830459",
            "user": "Stevecon",
            "timestamp": "19:16, 10 November 2015",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(16,512 bytes)",
            "change": 252,
            "summary": "(→‎Steps)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#*Certain compounds, like wood and glass, are poor conductors of electricity. Other materials, like seawater and many metals, conduct very well. The human body is able to conduct a current largely due to the amount of sodium and water in the body, and an electric shock happens when electricity flows through parts of the body.<ref>http://electronics.stackexchange.com/questions/152291/how-grounding-works-to-prevent-electrical-shock</ref>",
                    "#* There are essentially two types of electricians you can hire, “master” electricians and “journeyman” electricians. Both types are usually licensed by the state - but not always.  Master electricians generally own businesses and can employ other licensed electricians and helpers or apprentices, while journeyman electricians can work for a master electrician or be self-employed and hire a single helper or apprentice.  The rules of what each type of electrician may do and may not do varies by state, county or locality.",
                    "#Find out electricity requirements. The items and appliances in your household all have their own electrical requirements. Know the specific types of circuit breakers, fuses, and even lightbulbs that are required in your home. Make sure to replace them with the right parts when needed. Using incompatible parts could cause the equipment to function improperly, create an unsafe condition that may result in fire, injury or death.<ref>http://www.caes.uga.edu/departments/bae/extension/handbook/documents/Wiring%20Requirements%20for%20Household%20Circuits.pdf</ref> [[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Cover sockets and outlets. Covering outlets with wall panels is crucial for preventing accidental contact with wires, and is required by code. If you live with small children, it is also wise to use socket safety plugs to keep curious fingers safe from injury.<ref>http://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 3.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#*Certain compounds, like wood and glass, are poor conductors of electricity. Other materials, like water and many metals, conduct very well. The human body is able to conduct a current largely due to the amount of water in the body, and an electric shock happens when electricity flows through part of the body.<ref>http://electronics.stackexchange.com/questions/152291/how-grounding-works-to-prevent-electrical-shock</ref>",
                    "#* There are essentially two types of electricians you can hire, “master” electricians and “journeyman” electricians. Both types are licensed by the state, but master electricians can design electrical systems, while journeyman electricians can only install and fix electrical systems.<ref>http://www.thisoldhouse.com/toh/article/0,,213817,00.html</ref>",
                    "#Find out electricity requirements. The items and appliances in your household all have their own electrical requirements. Know the specific types of circuit breakers, fuses, and even lightbulbs that are required in your home. Make sure to replace them with the right parts when needed. Using incompatible parts could cause the equipment to function improperly or start an electrical fire.<ref>http://www.caes.uga.edu/departments/bae/extension/handbook/documents/Wiring%20Requirements%20for%20Household%20Circuits.pdf</ref> [[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Cover sockets and outlets. Covering outlets with wall panels is crucial for preventing accidental contact with wires. If you live with small children, it is also wise to use socket safety plugs to keep curious fingers safe from injury.<ref>http://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html</ref>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 3.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
