            "summary": "(Enlarging and centering Steps photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Learn how electricity works and why electric shock occurs. Knowledge is power, and the first step in preventing a dangerous situation is understanding the cause.<br><br>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Find out the electricity requirements of your home and household appliances. Know the specific types of circuit breakers, fuses, and even lightbulbs that are required in your home, and make sure to replace them with the right parts when needed. Using incompatible parts could cause the equipment to function improperly or start an electrical fire.<br><br>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Cover sockets and outlets. Covering outlets with wall panels is crucial for preventing accidental contact with wires. If you live with small children, it is also wise to use socket safety plugs to keep curious fingers safe from injury.<br><br>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Install Ground Fault Circuit Interrupter (GFCI) outlets and adapters. These devices are able to detect imbalances in the amount of electricity flowing through an appliance and will cut off power to the appliance. GFCI receptacles are required in most new construction homes, and can typically be installed in older homes for a low cost.<br><br>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Store and use electric devices away from water. Water and electricity do not mix well, and appliances should always be kept away from any moisture.<br><br>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 5.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Replace electrical equipment that is worn or damaged. Pay attention to the condition of your electric devices, and maintain them regularly. Some signs that indicate a need for repair are:<br><br>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 6.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Turn the power source off before working with electricity. Whenever a project involves the exposure of electrical equipment, check and double-check that the power is OFF before beginning your work.<br><br>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 7.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Wear protective gear. Rubber soled shoes and non-conductive gloves provide a barrier. Putting a rubber mat on the floor is another effective precaution.<br><br>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 8.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Exercise caution when operating power tools. Make sure that all of your tools have a three-prong plug, and inspect all equipment for signs of damage. It is also important to turn off power tools before connecting them to electricity. Always keep power tools away from water, and clear the work area of flamable gasses, vapors, and solvents when tools are in use.<br><br>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 9.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Call a professional for big jobs. Working with electricity is inherently dangerous and complicated. If you are not completely confident in your skills, bring in a trusted electrician to complete the job.<br><br>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 10.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Check the weather report. It may sound obvious, but making sure you have a clear forecast for your outdoor adventure is crucial to avoid getting caught in an electrical storm. Even if you're just gone for an afternoon, weather can change quickly and the best prevention is preparedness. Know the chance of a thunderstorm in the outdoor area you plan to visit, and plan to head in long before the [[Avoid Being Hit by Lightning|lightning]] is due to start.<br><br>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 11.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Watch for signs of a coming storm. Pay attention to changes in temperature, increases in wind, or darkening of the sky. Listen for thunder.<br><br>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 12.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Find shelter. If you are outside and a storm approaches, quickly heading indoors is the only real way to be protected from lightning. Look for a completely enclosed shelter with it's own electricity and plumbing, like a house or business. If that option is not available, hiding out in a car with the doors closed and windows up is also a safe bet. Covered picnic areas, stand-alone restroom facilities, tents, and other small structures will NOT keep you safe. No reliable shelter in sight? Reduce your risk with these preventive guidelines:<br><br>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 13.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Wait it out. Whether indoors or out, do not leave your chosen safety area for at least half an hour after the last clap of thunder is heard.<br><br>[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 14.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    ""
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 1.jpg|right|300px]]Learn how electricity works and why electric shock occurs. Knowledge is power, and the first step in preventing a dangerous situation is understanding the cause.",
                    "#[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 2.jpg|right|300px]]Find out the electricity requirements of your home and household appliances. Know the specific types of circuit breakers, fuses, and even lightbulbs that are required in your home, and make sure to replace them with the right parts when needed. Using incompatible parts could cause the equipment to function improperly or start an electrical fire.",
                    "#[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 3.jpg|right|300px]]Cover sockets and outlets. Covering outlets with wall panels is crucial for preventing accidental contact with wires. If you live with small children, it is also wise to use socket safety plugs to keep curious fingers safe from injury.",
                    "#[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 4.jpg|right|300px]]Install Ground Fault Circuit Interrupter (GFCI) outlets and adapters. These devices are able to detect imbalances in the amount of electricity flowing through an appliance and will cut off power to the appliance. GFCI receptacles are required in most new construction homes, and can typically be installed in older homes for a low cost.",
                    "#[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 5.jpg|right|300px]]Store and use electric devices away from water. Water and electricity do not mix well, and appliances should always be kept away from any moisture.",
                    "#[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 6.jpg|right|300px]]Replace electrical equipment that is worn or damaged. Pay attention to the condition of your electric devices, and maintain them regularly. Some signs that indicate a need for repair are:",
                    "#[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 7.jpg|right|300px]]Turn the power source off before working with electricity. Whenever a project involves the exposure of electrical equipment, check and double-check that the power is OFF before beginning your work.",
                    "#[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 8.jpg|right|300px]]Wear protective gear. Rubber soled shoes and non-conductive gloves provide a barrier. Putting a rubber mat on the floor is another effective precaution.",
                    "#[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 9.jpg|right|300px]]Exercise caution when operating power tools. Make sure that all of your tools have a three-prong plug, and inspect all equipment for signs of damage. It is also important to turn off power tools before connecting them to electricity. Always keep power tools away from water, and clear the work area of flamable gasses, vapors, and solvents when tools are in use.",
                    "#[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 10.jpg|right|300px]]Call a professional for big jobs. Working with electricity is inherently dangerous and complicated. If you are not completely confident in your skills, bring in a trusted electrician to complete the job.",
                    "",
                    "#[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 11.jpg|right|300px]]Check the weather report. It may sound obvious, but making sure you have a clear forecast for your outdoor adventure is crucial to avoid getting caught in an electrical storm. Even if you're just gone for an afternoon, weather can change quickly and the best prevention is preparedness. Know the chance of a thunderstorm in the outdoor area you plan to visit, and plan to head in long before the [[Avoid Being Hit by Lightning|lightning]] is due to start.",
                    "#[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 12.jpg|right|300px]]Watch for signs of a coming storm. Pay attention to changes in temperature, increases in wind, or darkening of the sky. Listen for thunder.",
                    "#[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 13.jpg|right|300px]]Find shelter. If you are outside and a storm approaches, quickly heading indoors is the only real way to be protected from lightning. Look for a completely enclosed shelter with it's own electricity and plumbing, like a house or business. If that option is not available, hiding out in a car with the doors closed and windows up is also a safe bet. Covered picnic areas, stand-alone restroom facilities, tents, and other small structures will NOT keep you safe. No reliable shelter in sight? Reduce your risk with these preventive guidelines:",
                    "#[[Image:Prevent Electrical Shock Step 14.jpg|right|300px]]Wait it out. Whether indoors or out, do not leave your chosen safety area for at least half an hour after the last clap of thunder is heard."
                ]
            }
        },
