        {
            "id": "10833992",
            "user": "Savannahcordova",
            "timestamp": "18:29, 16 August 2013",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(8,292 bytes)",
            "change": 1997,
            "summary": "(importing fellowship edits, will come back to fix formatting)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "cleanup",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "The inherent danger of electrical shock is no laughing matter, as it often results in serious injury and can even be fatal. However, there are a number of preventive safety measures you can employ to greatly reduce the risk, whether at home, on the job, or in the great outdoors. Read on to find out what you can do to prevent electric shock.",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "=== Preventing Electric Shock In Your Home ===",
                    "#'''Learn how electricity works and why electric shock occurs.''' Knowledge is power, and the first step in preventing a dangerous situation is understanding the cause.",
                    "#*In basic terms, [[Feel Safe Around Electricity|electricity]] naturally tries to flow to the earth or ground through any material that will conduct an electrical current.",
                    "#*Certain compounds, like wood and glass, are not good conductors of electricity. Other materials, like water and metal, work very well. The human body is able to conduct a current, and an electric shock happens when electricity flows through part of the body.",
                    "#*This happens most often when a direct source of electricity is exposed to human contact. It may also flow into a person through another conductor, like a pool of water or a metal pole.",
                    "#*To learn more about electricity and the cause of electric shock, read about it [http://www.howstuffworks.com/electricity.htm here] or ask a trusted electrician.",
                    "#'''Find out the electricity requirements of your home and household appliances.''' Know the specific types of circuit breakers, fuses, and even lightbulbs that are required in your home, and make sure to replace them with the right parts when needed. Using incompatible parts could cause the equipment to function improperly or start an electrical fire.",
                    "#'''Cover sockets and outlets.''' Covering outlets with wall panels is crucial for preventing accidental contact with wires. If you live with small children, it is also wise to use socket safety plugs to keep curious fingers safe from injury.",
                    "#'''Install Ground Fault Circuit Interrupter (GFCI) outlets and adapters.''' These devices are able to detect imbalances in the amount of electricity flowing through an appliance and will cut off power to the appliance. GFCI receptacles are required in most new construction homes, and can typically be installed in older homes for a low cost.",
                    "#'''Store and use electric devices away from water.''' Water and electricity do not mix well, and appliances should always be kept away from any moisture.",
                    "#*Never use an electrical appliance while in the bath or shower.",
                    "#*If your toaster or another electric appliance is near your kitchen sink, never use the running water and the appliance at the same time. Keep it unplugged when not in use.",
                    "#* Store outdoor electrical equipment in a place where they will be kept dry, like a garage shelf.",
                    "#*If a plugged-in appliance falls into water, do not attempt to retrieve it until you turn off power to the corresponding circuit. Once the power is off, you may retrieve the device. Once it is dry, it can be evaluated by an electrician to see if it is suitable for future use.",
                    "#'''Replace electrical equipment that is worn or damaged.''' Pay attention to the condition of your electric devices, and maintain them regularly. Some signs that indicate a need for repair are:",
                    "#*Sparking",
                    "#*Issuing of small shocks",
                    "#*Frayed or damaged cords",
                    "#*Heat from electrical outlets",
                    "#*Reccurent short-circuiting",
                    "These are only a few signs of wear and tear. If something else seems strange, contact an electrician. It's always better to be safe than sorry!",
                    "",
                    "=== Preventing Electric Shock On the Job===",
                    "#'''Turn the power source off before working with electricity.''' Whenever a project involves the exposure of electrical equipment, check and double-check that the power is OFF before beginning your work.",
                    "#'''Wear protective gear.''' Rubber soled shoes and non-conductive gloves provide a barrier. Putting a rubber mat on the floor is another effective precaution.",
                    "#'''Exercise caution when operating power tools.''' Make sure that all of your tools have a three-prong plug, and inspect all equipment for signs of damage. It is also important to turn off power tools before connecting them to electricity. Always keep power tools away from water, and clear the work area of flamable gasses, vapors, and solvents when tools are in use.",
                    "#'''Call a professional for big jobs.''' Working with electricity is inherently dangerous and complicated. If you are not completely confident in your skills, bring in a trusted electrician to complete the job.",
                    "",
                    "=== Preventing Electric Shock in a Lightning Storm===",
                    "#'''Check the weather report.''' It may sound obvious, but making sure you have a clear forecast for your outdoor adventure is crucial to avoid getting caught in an electrical storm. Even if you're just gone for an afternoon, weather can change quickly and the best prevention is preparedness. Know the chance of a thunderstorm in the outdoor area you plan to visit, and plan to head in long before the [[Avoid Being Hit by Lightning|lightning]] is due to start.",
                    "#'''Watch for signs of a coming storm.''' Pay attention to changes in temperature, increases in wind, or darkening of the sky. Listen for thunder.",
                    "#'''Find shelter.''' If you are outside and a storm approaches, quickly heading indoors is the only real way to be protected from lightning. Look for a completely enclosed shelter with it's own electricity and plumbing, like a house or business. If that option is not available, hiding out in a car with the doors closed and windows up is also a safe bet. Covered picnic areas, stand-alone restroom facilities, tents, and other small structures will NOT keep you safe. No reliable shelter in sight? Reduce your risk with these preventive guidelines:",
                    "#*Stay low",
                    "#*Avoid open areas",
                    "#*Avoid metal and water",
                    "#'''Wait it out.''' Whether indoors or out, do not leave your chosen safety area for at least half an hour after the last clap of thunder is heard.",
                    "*Never touch a bare wire that may be conducting a current.",
                    "*Avoid overloading power strips and other receptacles with multiple plugs. Using only two plugs per outlet lessens the risk of shock and fire.",
                    "*Utilize three-prong plugs whenever possible. The third prong, which grounds the electrical current, should never be removed.",
                    "*Keep a [[Use a Fire Extinguisher|fire extinguisher]] handy in areas where you work with electrical equipment. Fire extinguishers for use on electrical fires will have a \"C,\" \"BC,\" or \"ABC\" on the label.",
                    "*Never assume that someone else turned off the power source. Always check for yourself!",
                    "No matter how many precautions are in place, [[Treat a Victim of Electrical Shock|electric shock]] is always a risk when electricity is in use. If shock occurs, it is important to be prepared in order to handle the situation safely.",
                    "*In the case of an electric emergency, always call 911.",
                    "*Do not touch a victim of shock with bare hands, as they may still be conducting electricity. Use a non-conductive barrier, like rubber gloves, if at all possible.",
                    "*Turn off the power source if possible. If not, move the victim away from the source with a non-conductive material, like a piece of wood.",
                    "*Check to see if the person is breathing. If not, begin [[Do CPR on an Adult|CPR]] immediately.",
                    "*Keep the victim laying horizontally, with the legs slightly elevated.",
                    "*Wait for medical help to arrive.",
                    "*http://www.lightningsafety.noaa.gov/",
                    "*http://www.cableorganizer.com/articles/preventing-home-electrical-hazards.html",
                    "*http://www.safetyed.org/howtopreventelecrticalshock.html#.UWcIfJMpySo",
                    "*http://www.tva.gov/power/homesafety.htm",
                    "*http://home.howstuffworks.com/home-improvement/household-safety/tips/how-to-prevent-electrical-shock.htm",
                    "*http://www.ccohs.ca/oshanswers/safety_haz/electrical.html",
                    "*http://www.redbeacon.com/hg/5-ways-prevent-electrical-shock/",
                    "*http://www.webmd.com/a-to-z-guides/electric-shock"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "{{copyeditbot}}You can help prevent electric shocks by plugging in plastic outlet covers and if you are a responsible adult try and keep water and electricity as far away as possible, try and keep away from younger children.",
                    "#Know how it happens. Electrical shock happens when a part of your body provides a path to complete a circuit between different energized conductors or parts or to ground. The severity of electrical shock is dependent on the amount of current flow (amps or amperes) and the path through the victim's body. The higher the [[Calculate Total Resistance in Circuits|voltage]] or current; the greater the risk of shock, burns or even death.  ''Current flow of just .005 amps (5 milliamps) across the heart can stop it from beating.''",
                    "#Educate yourself. An ounce of electrical prevention is worth a ton of cure.  Know the following:<br><br>",
                    "#*''Voltage'' is the force that causes current to flow in a circuit.  Larger voltages can allow current to overcome significant resistance.",
                    "#*''Current'' is the amount of amperes (amps) that is flowing in a circuit.",
                    "#*''Resistance'' is the opposition to current flow in a circuit.  A ''conductor'' such as silver, copper and aluminum offers relatively little resistance; while an ''insulator'' such as rubber and glass offers significant resistance.",
                    "#*Circuits with large amounts of resistance (or low voltages) will have little current flow.  Reducing resistance or increasing voltage (or both) will quickly increase current flow.",
                    "#*A ''ground'' is usually a 0 volt reference point in an [[Replace a Defective Electrical Switch or Outlet|electrical circuit]] or power supply or system, and is often connected to and referenced as ''earth''.",
                    "#*Electricity flows in ''all'' resistance paths (high, low and every one in between) to ground.  It is not true that \"electricity ''only'' takes the path of least resistance to ground\"",
                    "#Be safe. Here are a few tips to follow:<br><br>",
                    "#*Never overload electrical circuits.",
                    "#*Always check electrical cords for fraying, signs of wear, damaged jacket or insulation and defects before use.",
                    "#*Always shut off the circuit breakers or remove fuses when working on equipment or wiring.  Lock the panel shut and / or tape a note to it to let others know a circuit is being worked upon.",
                    "#*Keep all electric equipment and wiring away from dampness and water.",
                    "#*Inspect your area for all potential hazards - not just [[Prevent Electrical Accidents with Hand Tools|electrical hazards]] before beginning to work.",
                    "#*Some low voltages will not kill (especially if of a short duration), but can easily lead to injury or even death.  This can happen if becoming startled by contact with an energized part or a seeing a spark while working above on a ladder that results in a fall, or causes muscles in your hand to tighten and grip an energized wire or part - making it impossible to let go.",
                    "#*Test all GFCI / GFI (Ground Fault Circuit Interrupter) circuit breakers and receptacles monthly by pressing the \"TEST\" then \"RESET\" button.",
                    "#*Test all AFCI (Arc Fault Circuit Interrupter) circuit breakers monthly by pressing the \"TEST\" then \"RESET\" button.",
                    "#*Connect electrical appliances, devices and tools to GFCI protected circuits if they will be used in or on: garages, kitchen counters, bathrooms, concrete or dirt floor basements, patios, outdoors, porches, decks and any other area that is or can become damp or wet.",
                    "#*Connect outdoor electrical devices and tools only to outlets that are protected by ''Ground Fault Interrupter'' circuit breakers and receptacles if they lack such protection on the power cord itself.",
                    "#*Never stand on or come into contact with an aluminum ladder when using electric tools, especially in the places mentioned above using GFCI circuits.",
                    "#*Never stand on or come into contact with an aluminum ladder used on a building with aluminum or steel siding.",
                    "#*Never defeat or remove the ground pin on a cord.",
                    "#*In case of an electrical fire, shut off power (if possible) and discharge a ''Class C'' fire extinguisher (electrical fires) at the base of the fire.  Class C fire extinguishers do not conduct electricity back to the user.",
                    "#*Never use water, Class A or Class B fire extinguishers to put out an electrical fire! To do so could result in a fatal shock.",
                    "#Understand that to prevent the risk of an electrical shock, you must make sure that your body does not become energized ''and'' becomes a pathway to another energized part or wire or to ground.",
                    "*Discard or replace any worn or defective electrical cords.",
                    "*Contact a qualified (often called \"licensed\") electrician to do electrical work that you are unsure about doing.  Some codes may mandate use of qualified electricians for some types of work or in certain locations.",
                    "*When working on any electrical equipment, check, then double check, to be sure there is no source of electricity flowing.",
                    "*Never assume that someone else has cut off the power source. Check for yourself.",
                    "*Install GFCI (USA) or RCBO (EU) - Ground Fault Circuit Interrupter  / Residual Current Circuit Breaker with Over-current Protection in mains and in bathrooms, unfinished basements, garages, outdoors, (consult local code or code enforcement officials for additional information). These dvices will automatically open power if you contacted energized wires or devices. Don't ignore this as it is very, very important to your personal safety.",
                    "*Keep a [[Use a Fire Extinguisher|fire extinguisher]] handy in areas where you work with electrical equipment.",
                    "*Fire extinguishers for use on electrical fires will be labeled as a C, BC, or ABC extinguisher.",
                    "*Do not work alone.  Make sure someone (spouse, helper, friend, etc.) is close by and can check to make sure you haven't been stricken.",
                    "*Avoid contact with electrical equipment in damp or wet areas.",
                    "*http://www.cdc.gov/nasd/docs/d001601-d001700/d001684/d001684.html"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
